'''''Glandora diffusa''''', the '''purple gromwell''', [[synonym (taxonomy)|syn.]] ''Lithodora diffusa'', ''Lithospermum diffusa'', is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Boraginaceae. It is a mat-forming [[perennial plant|perennial]] growing to {{convert|15|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall by {{convert|60|cm|0|abbr=on}} or more wide, with dark green, hairy [[evergreen]] leaves and masses of blue or white 5-lobed flowers. It is suitable for cultivation in a [[rock garden]] or [[alpine garden]].<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|pages=1136}}</ref>

Although there is a variety with white flowers, ''G. diffusa'' var. ''alba'',<ref name=":0">{{Cite book|title=Herbaceous Perennial Plants|last=Armitage|first=Allan|publisher=Varsity Press, Inc.|year=1989|isbn=9780942375015|location=Athens, GA|pages=385–386}}</ref> the blue-flowered varieties are most valued in gardens for the intense azure color of their abundant blooms.  A number of [[cultivars]] have been developed for garden use.<ref>{{cite web | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/Search-Results?form-mode=true&query=Glandora%20diffusa | title = results for 'Glandora diffusa' | date = July 2017  | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 6 September 2020 }}</ref> 

''Glandora diffusa'' is hardy down to {{convert|-23|C|F|abbr=on}} ([[Hardiness zone|USDA zones]] 6 - 8),<ref name=":0" /> and requires a position in full sun. It needs a well-drained acid or neutral soil, as it is susceptible to root rot.  It requires [[vernalization]] to flower.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.botanicsinternational.co.uk/data-sheets/blooms/Lithodora%20White%20Star&#91;1&#93;.pdf|title=Lithodora diffusa 'White Star' Technical Guide|publisher=Blooms of Bressingham|access-date=2017-01-07|archive-date=2017-02-24|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170224093243/http://botanicsinternational.co.uk/data-sheets/blooms/Lithodora%20White%20Star%5B1%5D.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The ancient Greek term ''lithodora'' literally means "stone gift", referring to its preferred habitat. ''Diffusa'' means "spreading", and refers to the plant's growth habit.<ref name=AZPLA>{{cite book|last=Coombes|first=Allen J.|title=The A to Z of plant names|year=2012|publisher=Timber Press|location=USA|isbn=9781604691962|pages=[https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781604691962/page/312 312]|url-access=registration|url=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781604691962/page/312}}</ref>

